From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2026 04:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 69/69] local: iommu/arm-smmu: reprogram the SMMU on hibernate
 restore

The same defect patch 0041 fixed for SMMUv3, in the v2 driver, and the reason
the GPU stays dead after a cross-boot hibernate restore on x1e80100 at EL2.

arm_smmu_pm_ops uses SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(), so .restore is an alias of
arm_smmu_pm_resume(), which begins:

	ret = clk_bulk_prepare(smmu->num_clks, smmu->clks);
	if (ret)
		return ret;
	if (pm_runtime_suspended(dev))
		return 0;

That early return is right for s2idle: one kernel owns the hardware throughout,
and a runtime-suspended SMMU genuinely has nothing to restore. It is wrong for a
hibernate restore, where "runtime-suspended" is a property of the restored IMAGE
rather than of the silicon. The reboot reset the SMMU; the stream mappings,
context banks and io-pgtables came back from the image; and because the GPU is
idle when hibernation freezes devices its SMMU is runtime-suspended, so
arm_smmu_device_reset() never runs and the hardware is never told any of it.

Evidence. Holding the GPU runtime-active across the hibernate (sysfs
power/control=on) stops adreno's .restore being a 5 us no-op and makes it run
the real GMU bring-up inside dpm_resume, where it fails with -110.
Instrumentation then eliminated the two obvious causes: pm_runtime_get_sync()
on the GMU succeeds, and reading the RSC sequencer uCode straight back after
writing it returns 0xeaaae5a0 as expected -- so the CX domain is powered and the
RSCC is both writable and readable. The following bring-up takes the warm path,
skips a6xx_rpmh_start() entirely, and fails with "GMU firmware initialization
timed out": the GMU cannot fetch its firmware. That is a DMA failure, not a
power or register-programming one.

Add a .restore that forces arm_smmu_device_reset(), spelling out dev_pm_ops so
.restore can differ from .resume. Clocks are left exactly as
arm_smmu_pm_resume() leaves them -- prepared and enabled when the device is
runtime-active, prepared only when it is not -- so runtime PM accounting is
unchanged. Ordering relies on the iommu device links placing the SMMU ahead of
its masters in the dpm list; 0041 needed .restore_noirq only because it sits
ahead of a PCIe root complex.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu.c | 50 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 50 insertions(+), 1 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu.c
+++ b/drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu.c
@@ -2348,8 +2348,57 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Hibernation restore. The reboot reset the SMMU, but our stream mappings,
+ * context banks and io-pgtables all came back from the image, so the hardware
+ * knows nothing about them until arm_smmu_device_reset() reprograms it.
+ *
+ * arm_smmu_pm_resume() cannot be reused here: it returns early whenever the
+ * device is runtime-suspended, which it is whenever its masters are idle. That
+ * is correct for s2idle, where one kernel owns the hardware throughout and a
+ * runtime-suspended SMMU genuinely has nothing to restore. It is wrong for a
+ * restore, where "runtime-suspended" describes the image rather than the
+ * silicon: the reset is skipped, the hardware is never reprogrammed, and every
+ * master behind it faults or hangs. On x1e80100 that master is the GPU -- the
+ * GMU cannot fetch its firmware and a6xx_gmu_fw_start() times out.
+ *
+ * Force the reset, leaving the clocks exactly as arm_smmu_pm_resume() would so
+ * runtime PM accounting is unchanged.
+ */
+static int __maybe_unused arm_smmu_pm_restore(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct arm_smmu_device *smmu = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = clk_bulk_prepare(smmu->num_clks, smmu->clks);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = clk_bulk_enable(smmu->num_clks, smmu->clks);
+	if (ret) {
+		clk_bulk_unprepare(smmu->num_clks, smmu->clks);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	arm_smmu_device_reset(smmu);
+
+	if (pm_runtime_suspended(dev))
+		clk_bulk_disable(smmu->num_clks, smmu->clks);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Spelled out rather than using SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS() so .restore can
+ * differ from .resume; that macro aliases all six onto the suspend/resume pair.
+ */
 static const struct dev_pm_ops arm_smmu_pm_ops = {
-	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(arm_smmu_pm_suspend, arm_smmu_pm_resume)
+	.suspend = pm_sleep_ptr(arm_smmu_pm_suspend),
+	.resume = pm_sleep_ptr(arm_smmu_pm_resume),
+	.freeze = pm_sleep_ptr(arm_smmu_pm_suspend),
+	.thaw = pm_sleep_ptr(arm_smmu_pm_resume),
+	.poweroff = pm_sleep_ptr(arm_smmu_pm_suspend),
+	.restore = pm_sleep_ptr(arm_smmu_pm_restore),
 	SET_RUNTIME_PM_OPS(arm_smmu_runtime_suspend,
 			   arm_smmu_runtime_resume, NULL)
 };
